    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you maybe expand a bit more on the \"instability\" risk with vertical scaling? Like, if we just keep making one server bigger and bigger, what's the actual downside from a technical standpoint? I want to make sure they understand why we can't just keep upgrading the same instance.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Physically increasing a single server's capacity hits hardware limits, eventually forcing a complete stabilization or migration to a new node. Furthermore, horizontal scaling prevents this sudden instability by distributing traffic across multiple independent instances, thereby eliminating single points of failure and ensuring that if one server slows down or fails, the application remains operational on the others. This architectural resilience is why relying solely on vertical scaling becomes unsustainable for enterprises facing unpredictable traffic spikes.",
      "source": "target",
